(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 61-year-old man has sustained ventricular tachycardia while admitted with a severe infection and is too unwell for device implantation now. Which device can detect and shock a lethal ventricular rhythm in the meantime?","choices":["Wearable defibrillator vest","Ambulatory Holter monitor","Implantable loop recorder","Transcutaneous pacing pads"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A wearable (external) cardioverter-defibrillator, the LifeVest, detects fatal arrhythmias and delivers a shock without surgery, so it protects this man from sudden cardiac death while he is too unwell for an implantable device. The implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) does the same job once it can be placed. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has bothersome palpitations. Her echocardiogram is normal, a 24-hour monitor shows premature ventricular contractions in 3% of beats, she has never fainted, and her electrolytes and oxygen saturation are normal. She has already stopped caffeine. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Implantable defibrillator","Amiodarone","Beta blocker","Catheter ablation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her burden of 3% is below the 15% high-risk line, her echocardiogram is normal and she has never fainted, so she is low risk, but she has symptoms. With electrolytes, oxygen and caffeine already addressed, a beta blocker is first-line; catheter ablation follows only if symptoms are refractory or the drug is not tolerated. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman collapses in a hospital hallway. The monitor shows chaotic, uncoordinated activity, and she has no pulse. Compressions have started and defibrillator pads are attached. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Amiodarone before any shock","Check electrolytes first","Defibrillate now","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chaotic, uncoordinated activity with no pulse is ventricular fibrillation, treated as cardiac arrest. With compressions underway and pads attached, she is defibrillated (unsynchronized cardioversion) as soon as possible, followed by 2 minutes of cardiopulmonary resuscitation and epinephrine every 3 to 5 minutes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man collapses in a gym. He is unresponsive, not breathing and has no pulse. The monitor shows chaotic, irregular waves of varying size with no identifiable QRS complexes. Which rhythm is present?","choices":["Asystole","Pulseless electrical activity","Ventricular fibrillation","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular fibrillation has no pulse and no perfusion, a rate of 0, no rhythm, no P waves and no PR interval, only uncoordinated activity in place of QRS complexes. Chaotic waves of varying size in an unresponsive, pulseless man fit exactly, and he needs defibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman in cardiac arrest has an organized sinus rhythm on the monitor but no pulse. A team member begins to charge the defibrillator. Which statement about treating this rhythm is accurate?","choices":["It needs a synchronized shock","It needs transcutaneous pacing","It should not be shocked","It needs defibrillation now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An organized sinus rhythm with no pulse is pulseless electrical activity, which, like asystole, is unshockable. Chest compressions start immediately, early intubation is considered, and high-quality compressions with epinephrine every 3 to 5 minutes are the mainstays; hypovolemia and hypoxia are the two leading causes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man is cardioverted from unstable sustained ventricular tachycardia. He is now stable in sinus rhythm, and no reversible cause is found. Which is the most appropriate plan before discharge?","choices":["Lifelong oral magnesium","Implantable defibrillator","Permanent pacemaker","No further therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He survived unstable sustained ventricular tachycardia with no reversible cause, so once stabilized he needs an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death related to life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension and diabetes has two spells of lightheadedness this week. Her electrocardiogram shows regular P waves, a constant PR interval of 0.16 seconds in the conducted beats, and occasional P waves with no QRS complex after them, with no lengthening of the PR beforehand. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Complete heart block","Mobitz type II block","Mobitz type I block","First-degree block"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Regular P waves, a constant PR of 0.16 seconds in conducted beats and occasional P waves with no QRS, with no PR lengthening beforehand, is Mobitz type II block. Her lightheaded spells fit symptoms of bradycardia, and her long-standing hypertension and diabetes fit its most common cause, His-Purkinje disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with acute chest pain develops polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (QRS complexes that vary in shape) with a normal QT and is defibrillated back to sinus rhythm. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent coronary angiography","Outpatient stress test","Pericardiocentesis","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a normal QT is most commonly due to acute myocardial ischemia, and she had acute chest pain. After defibrillation, the next step is urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization; intravenous metoprolol or amiodarone may be added. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has an electrocardiogram before a sports tryout. The QRS is 0.14 seconds, with an rsR' pattern (a small upward wave, a dip, then a taller second upward wave) in V1 to V3 and a slurred S wave in leads I and aVL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Accessory pathway","Ventricular tachycardia","Right bundle branch block","Left bundle branch block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A QRS over 0.12 seconds (here 0.14), an rsR' pattern ('bunny ears') in V1, V2 and V3 and a slurred S wave in I and aVL are the criteria for right bundle branch block. The right ventricle is activated late from across the septum, and new block can occur without any underlying heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 24-year-old marathon runner has an electrocardiogram at a sports physical. He has no symptoms. The PR interval grows longer with each beat until one P wave is not followed by a QRS complex, and then the cycle repeats. The QRS complexes are narrow. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mobitz type I block","Mobitz type II block","First-degree block","Complete heart block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A PR interval that grows longer with each beat until a P wave is not conducted, then repeats, with narrow QRS complexes, is Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) block. In a symptom-free marathon runner, increased vagal tone from an overreactive vagus nerve is a named cause, and the block needs no treatment. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman learns her electrocardiogram shows a constant PR interval of 0.22 seconds with every beat conducted. She feels well and asks at what point this kind of delay usually starts to cause symptoms. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Once the PR exceeds 0.12 seconds","When a QRS starts dropping","Once the PR exceeds 0.3 seconds","Once the PR exceeds 0.20 seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her constant PR of 0.22 seconds is first-degree block, and the vast majority of such patients have no symptoms. Fatigue, shortness of breath, palpitations and chest pain usually appear once the PR interval exceeds 0.3 seconds, the point at which a permanent pacemaker is also considered. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man who takes metoprolol, atorvastatin, aspirin and lisinopril has a heart rate of 48 and develops torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline) from a drug-induced long QT. Which of his medications worsens this rhythm through an indirect effect on the QT?","choices":["Metoprolol","Atorvastatin","Aspirin","Lisinopril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His heart rate of 48 reflects his beta blocker, and beta blockers are avoided in torsades de pointes because the bradycardia they cause lengthens the R-to-R interval and so indirectly prolongs the QT. Magnesium breaks the rhythm; amiodarone and procainamide are avoided for direct QT prolongation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is in sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia at 150 per minute. Her blood pressure is 124/76 and she is alert, but she reports new chest pain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Defibrillation","Observation and labs","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Instability in ventricular tachycardia is defined by any one of chest pain, altered mental status, shortness of breath or hypotension. Her new chest pain makes her unstable despite a blood pressure of 124/76, and with a pulse and a monomorphic rhythm she gets synchronized cardioversion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a 24-hour monitor for an unrelated reason. It shows premature ventricular contractions in under 1% of beats. He has no symptoms, and his echocardiogram is normal. Which plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Daily beta blocker","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator","Reassurance, remove triggers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A burden under 1% (fewer than 1,000 a day), a normal echocardiogram and no symptoms make him low risk, so the plan is reassurance and eliminating triggers such as alcohol, caffeine and stimulants. A beta blocker would be first-line only if symptoms appeared. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with known complete heart block awaiting device placement reports two fainting episodes this week. Why is this symptom especially concerning?","choices":["It means the block is resolving","It points to a seizure disorder","It suggests low blood glucose","It signals higher sudden death risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms of complete block range from none to severe, and syncope is singled out because it is associated with an increased risk of sudden death. Two faints in a week while awaiting a device make her case more urgent; the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker, with transcutaneous pacing as the emergent bridge. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman in a regular wide QRS tachycardia with identical complexes suddenly becomes unresponsive, and no pulse can be felt. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Vagal maneuvers","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous lidocaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Unresponsive with no pulse in a regular wide tachycardia with identical complexes is pulseless monomorphic ventricular tachycardia. The first step is to check for a pulse; when it is absent, the patient is defibrillated (unsynchronized cardioversion), with the remaining steps the same as for ventricular fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with no symptoms and normal exercise tolerance has a routine electrocardiogram showing a constant PR interval of 0.24 seconds, with every P wave conducted to a narrow QRS complex. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Transcutaneous pacing","Oral beta blocker","No treatment needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her constant PR of 0.24 seconds with every P wave conducted to a narrow QRS is first-degree block, and she has no symptoms. No treatment is needed unless debilitating or heart failure symptoms develop, and a permanent pacemaker is considered only for symptoms or a PR over 0.3 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a long QT has recurrent runs of torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). Between runs he is alert, with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the first treatment?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous metoprolol","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Between runs he is alert with a pulse and a normal blood pressure, so his torsades de pointes is treated with intravenous magnesium to break it, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. Amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided; lidocaine is the one antiarrhythmic that can be used. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man who injects drugs is being treated for aortic valve endocarditis. On day 4 his heart rate falls to 38, and the electrocardiogram shows P waves with no relationship to wide QRS complexes. Which complication of his infection explains the new rhythm?","choices":["Septic emboli to the lungs","Splinter hemorrhages","Drug-induced vagal tone","A perivalvular abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. On day 4 of aortic valve endocarditis his rate fell to 38, with P waves unrelated to wide QRS complexes: complete block. Bacterial endocarditis with an associated abscess is a named cause of complete block, and the rhythm is treated with pacing, then a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with no heart symptoms has a right bundle branch block on a routine electrocardiogram. She has no lung disease, no recent immobility and a normal echocardiogram. Which named cause best fits her?","choices":["Age-related degeneration","Pulmonary embolism","Myocarditis","Pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system is a named cause of right bundle branch block. At 84, with no symptoms, no lung disease, no immobility and a normal echocardiogram, the other listed causes are unlikely, and treatment of the block depends on any underlying condition. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a cardiac arrest after choking on food at a restaurant. The obstruction is removed, but he remains pulseless with an organized rhythm on the monitor. Compressions are underway. Which airway step should be considered early?","choices":["Wait for a pulse first","A nebulized bronchodilator","Intubation","Defibrillation first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pulseless patient with an organized rhythm has pulseless electrical activity, which is unshockable. Compressions start immediately and intubation is considered early; hypoxia, as from choking, is one of its two leading causes in adults, with hypovolemia, and epinephrine every 3 to 5 minutes is a mainstay. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive lung disease is admitted with an exacerbation. She is confused, and the monitor shows runs of skipped beats from frequent early, wide QRS complexes without P waves. Her arterial oxygen tension is low. Which underlying problem should be corrected first?","choices":["Her hypoxemia","Her blood pressure","Her blood glucose","Her body temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Early, wide QRS complexes without P waves are premature ventricular contractions, and hypoxemia is a named cause, along with electrolyte imbalance. Her chronic obstructive lung disease exacerbation, confusion and low arterial oxygen tension make hypoxemia the problem to correct first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman has a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids, and her electrocardiogram shows a left bundle branch block. Which of her conditions is singled out as a cause of this block?","choices":["Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Valvular heart disease, especially aortic stenosis, is singled out as a cause of left bundle branch block, and her harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids is the aortic stenosis murmur. Other listed causes are chronic hypertension, ischemic heart disease and dilated cardiomyopathy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with complete heart block is stabilized with transcutaneous pacing in the emergency department. Which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Oral beta blocker","Permanent pacemaker","Implantable defibrillator","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In complete (third-degree) block no P waves conduct, and the ventricles rely on a slow escape rhythm. Atropine, epinephrine and transcutaneous pacing are the emergent measures that stabilize him; the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after a motorcycle crash with a large thigh wound and heavy blood loss. He has no pulse. The monitor shows a regular narrow-complex rhythm at 110 per minute. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Asystole","Sinus tachycardia","Pulseless electrical activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow-complex rhythm at 110, one you would expect to produce a pulse, with no pulse is pulseless electrical activity. His heavy blood loss fits hypovolemia, one of its two leading causes in adults along with hypoxia, and the rhythm is unshockable, treated with compressions and epinephrine. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with symptomatic premature ventricular contractions and a structurally normal heart stopped her beta blocker after two weeks because of fatigue and dizziness. Her symptoms persist. Which is the most appropriate next option?","choices":["Reassurance and follow-up","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a low-risk patient with symptomatic premature ventricular contractions and a structurally normal heart, a beta blocker is first-line. She could not tolerate it (fatigue, dizziness) and her symptoms persist, so catheter ablation, used when symptoms are refractory or drug therapy is not tolerated, is next. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 26-year-old healthy man has an incidental right bundle branch block on a pre-employment electrocardiogram. His examination and echocardiogram are normal, and he asks whether this means he has heart disease. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["It can occur without heart disease","It proves a past heart attack","It requires a pacemaker","It will become complete block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A new right bundle branch block is not always pathological and may occur in people with no apparent underlying heart disease. With a normal examination and echocardiogram, this young man can be reassured; treatment depends on any underlying condition, and the block itself is usually asymptomatic. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with known Mobitz type II block suddenly collapses while gardening and regains consciousness within a minute; her husband saw brief jerking of her arms. Her heart rate is 36. Which term describes this event?","choices":["Transient ischemic attack","Stokes-Adams attack","Vasovagal syncope","Absence seizure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A Stokes-Adams attack is a sudden fainting spell or seizure from a rapid, temporary drop in blood flow to the brain, and Mobitz type II can lead to it. Her sudden collapse with brief arm jerking, quick recovery and rate of 36 fit; the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with crushing chest pain develops a wide QRS tachycardia whose complexes vary in shape from beat to beat; his QT before the episode was normal. He is still talking and has a pulse. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone alone","Observation on the monitor","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A wide tachycardia with complexes that vary in shape is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, and with a normal QT and crushing chest pain, acute ischemia is the likely driver. It is never truly stable, so he is defibrillated even with a pulse, then sent for urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is admitted with an acute myocardial infarction. He has never had a cardiac procedure and has had no tick bites. On the second hospital day his monitor shows a PR interval that lengthens with each beat until a QRS complex is dropped, in a repeating pattern. Which named cause of this rhythm fits him best?","choices":["Age-related conduction system wear","Atrioventricular node ischemia","A recent cardiac ablation","Lyme carditis from a tick bite"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A PR that lengthens until a QRS drops, in a repeating pattern, is Mobitz type I (Wenckebach). Atrioventricular node ischemia, usually after a myocardial infarction, is a named cause, and his block appeared on day 2 of an acute infarction, while the stem rules out ablation and Lyme carditis. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 6, 9"}]};
